SoraMech Language Drivers
=========================

Each file here is a driver script. The SoraMech runner invokes any driver
identically, regardless of language. Adding support for a new language means
adding one script to this directory and one entry in drivers.json.

Contract
--------

The runner calls a driver as:

  <driver-script> <file-path> <fn-name> <arg-count> [<arg> ...]

  file-path   absolute or relative path to the source file
  fn-name     name of the function to call within that file
  arg-count   number of arguments that follow
  args        zero or more argument strings; structured values are JSON strings

On success:
  - exit code 0
  - stdout: a single JSON value (string, number, boolean, object, or null)
    each box has exactly one output wire; whatever the function returns is
    encoded as one JSON value and passed downstream

On failure:
  - exit code non-zero
  - stderr: human-readable error message
  - stdout: empty or ignored

Built-in drivers
----------------

  lua.sh    — calls a function in a .lua module via luajit
  bash.sh   — sources a .sh file and calls a bash function
  c.sh      — compiles a .c file (caches binary in tmp/cache/) and runs it

Registering a driver
--------------------

In the map's drivers.json:

  { ".rs": "drivers/rust.sh" }

Write drivers/rust.sh following the contract above. The runner has no
knowledge of any language — it reads the extension, finds the script, calls it.

Writing a driver
----------------

Minimum viable driver (bash pseudocode):

  FILE="${1}"   FN="${2}"   N="${3}"   shift 3
  # compile / interpret FILE if needed
  # invoke FN with "$@" as arguments
  # print result as a single JSON value to stdout
  # exit 0 on success, non-zero on failure

The arg-count field is informational. Drivers may use "$@" directly.
